58:Once a project has been saved it autosaves in place, the macOS way: changes are written to the package as you work, and the package is written beside the old one and swapped in, so a crash part-way through a save cannot leave half a package behind. Because of this, macOS adds its standard document commands to the File menu: **Duplicate**, **Rename…**, **Move To…**, **Revert To ▸** (in place of Revert to Saved) and **Share**, and the same Rename and Move To controls appear when you click the window's title. A project only ever saves as a `.framejob` package. Making a movie or a still is a separate step; see [Export](export.md).

// MARK: - Writing

/// The package: `project.json` written by the CORE (nothing re-encodes a
/// `ProjectSnapshot` — the core owns the file format) plus the
/// regenerable `cache/` directory, carried across so a save does not
/// throw away every filmstrip.
override func fileWrapper(ofType typeName: String) throws -> FileWrapper {
    guard let data = project.json.data(using: .utf8) else {
        throw ReelCoreError(message: "The project could not be encoded.")
    }
    let json = FileWrapper(regularFileWithContents: data)
    json.preferredFilename = Self.projectFileName
    var children = [Self.projectFileName: json]
    if let cache = cacheWrapper() { children[Self.cacheDirectoryName] = cache }
    // Consolidated media, carried across the same way — and it is not
    // optional the way the cache is: this wrapper is a whitelist, so a
    // `media/` left out here is a `media/` DELETED by the next save
    // (autosave included), taking the only copy of the footage with it.
    if let media = directoryWrapper(Self.mediaDirectoryName) {
        children[Self.mediaDirectoryName] = media
    }
    if let graphics = graphicsWrapper() { children[Self.graphicsDirectoryName] = graphics }
    return FileWrapper(directoryWithFileWrappers: children)
}

/// The existing cache directory as a LAZY wrapper: its bytes are read
/// while NSDocument writes the new package to its temporary location,
/// which is before the swap that replaces the old one — so the source
/// files are still there. Reading eagerly instead would pull every
/// thumbnail into memory on every autosave.
private func cacheWrapper() -> FileWrapper? {
    guard let directory = existingCacheDirectory,
          let wrapper = try? FileWrapper(url: directory, options: [])
    else { return nil }
    wrapper.preferredFilename = Self.cacheDirectoryName
    return wrapper
}

/// Any other directory of the package, as a lazy wrapper — `media/`
/// today. Same reasoning as `cacheWrapper` for the laziness, and the
/// same nil-when-absent answer; unlike the cache this one only ever
/// lives inside a saved package, so an untitled document has none.
private func directoryWrapper(_ name: String) -> FileWrapper? {
    guard let fileURL = fileURL else { return nil }
    let directory = fileURL.appendingPathComponent(name, isDirectory: true)
    guard FileManager.default.fileExists(atPath: directory.path),
          let wrapper = try? FileWrapper(url: directory, options: [])
    else { return nil }
    wrapper.preferredFilename = name
    return wrapper
}

/// Save/Save As on an untitled document opens next to the file it was
/// built from, named after it — spec §1's "Save then writes a `.framejob`
/// package next to the source".
override func prepareSavePanel(_ savePanel: NSSavePanel) -> Bool {
    if let source = importSourceURL {
        savePanel.directoryURL = source.deletingLastPathComponent()
        savePanel.nameFieldStringValue = source.deletingPathExtension().lastPathComponent
    }
    return super.prepareSavePanel(savePanel)
}

override func defaultDraftName() -> String {
    importSourceURL?.deletingPathExtension().lastPathComponent ?? "Untitled Project"
}

// MARK: - Media

/// Every media entry that resolves to a file on disk. `Playback` and
/// `CompositionBuilder` take this; an id missing from it is missing
/// media and renders as a slate (spec §5.6).
func mediaURLs() -> [UInt32: URL] {
    var urls: [UInt32: URL] = [:]
    for media in project.snapshot.media {
        if let url = mediaURL(for: media) { urls[media.id] = url }
    }
    return urls
}

/// Media whose file is gone. The Media tab badges these and offers
/// Relink…; the project opened anyway.
var missingMedia: [MediaSnapshot] {
    project.snapshot.media.filter { mediaURL(for: $0) == nil }
}

/// Where `MediaImporter` writes thumbs and peaks: inside the package once
/// the document has a file, a per-document temporary directory before
/// that (`fileWrapper(ofType:)` copies it in on the first save). Caches
/// orphaned there regenerate on demand — except a transcript still owing
/// AssemblyAI a DELETE, whose only record goes with it (§10.13, R3·2).
var cacheDirectory: URL? {
    guard let directory = intendedCacheDirectory else { return nil }
    let existed = FileManager.default.fileExists(atPath: directory.path)
    let before = existed ? nil : fileURL.flatMap { try? URL(fileURLWithPath: $0.path)
        .resourceValues(forKeys: [.contentModificationDateKey]).contentModificationDate }
    try? FileManager.default.createDirectory(at: directory, withIntermediateDirectories: true)
    if let before, before == fileModificationDate { packageContentsDidChangeOnDisk() }
    return directory
}

# 2. The six ideas, and why the model stays simple

Six ideas do most of the work. Frame Job is built on all six.

1. **One kind of track.** Every track holds any clip — video, audio,
   still, text, shape. There are no video-vs-audio track types; an
   audio-only clip simply draws a waveform. Higher track = in front.
2. **A fixed canvas with direct manipulation.** The document has a size
   (1920×1080 by default). Clips are placed on it by dragging on the
   preview; scale, position, rotation and crop are clip properties with
   numbers in the inspector and handles on the canvas.
3. **Actions, not keyframes.** An Action is a span on a clip with an
   easing curve. You edit the property values at the *end* of the span;
   the values at the start are whatever was already in effect. There is
   no keyframe graph.
4. **Overlap is a transition.** Sliding one clip over its neighbour on
   the same track makes a transition of that length. No transition
   track, no browser to drag from.
5. **One inspector, tabbed by what the selection is.** Video / Audio /
   Text / Shape for the selected clip; Media and Document always.
6. **A small keyboard vocabulary.** ⌘T split, W/E trim to playhead,
   ⌘⌫ ripple delete, ⇧⌘F freeze frame, M marker, Space/JKL transport.

Left out: everything that only makes sense for a recording
it made itself (mouse/keyboard/touch callouts, pointer effects), templates,
publishing to YouTube/Vimeo, the stock-media library.
//! `nest` and `unnest` — grouping (spec §6, table "Clips").
//!
//! The two halves of one idea: a selection of clips becomes ONE clip on the
//! timeline showing a [`Nest`], and a nest clip becomes the clips again.
//! Their own module because grouping is a different concern from placement
//! — no other op changes which CONTAINER a clip lives in — and because the
//! pair only makes sense read together.
//!
//! ### They are inverses, up to ids
//!
//! `nest` then `unnest` gives back the clips it took: same sources, same
//! relative times, same props, same actions, same stacking. The ids differ,
//! exactly as they do across `split` then a re-join, because `unnest` mints
//! fresh ones — it has to, since a second clip may still show the same nest
//! and the clips inside it cannot be in two places at once.
//!
//! Two asymmetries are deliberate, and both come from the schema:
//!
//! - **A trimmed nest clip gives everything back.** `Source::Nest` has no in
//!   point (spec §5.2), so `unnest` is the inverse of `nest`, not of the
//!   trim that followed it: content the nest clip's edges were hiding
//!   reappears rather than being destroyed. Nothing is lost, and the user
//!   can trim again.
//! - **A clip the overlap rule refuses on the way out goes on a new track in
//!   front**, the fallback `paste` and Add Text already use (spec §6): a
//!   round trip lands on the tracks it came from because the nest clip's own
//!   span is free once it is removed, and only a nest that has since been
//!   moved next to other material needs the fallback. Refusing the whole op
//!   would leave the user with a group they cannot open.
//!
//! ### What `nest` decides
//!
//! The nest clip lands on the FRONT-most track the selection occupied that
//! is not a matte track (the front-most when all are, spec §11 MT-5), which
//! is where the selection's front-most DRAWN layer already was, and runs from
//! the earliest start to the latest end. The nest's own canvas is frozen at
//! the project's current size, so a later `set_canvas` cannot silently re-lay
//! out what is inside it ([`Nest`]). Its tracks are fresh — new ids, the
//! source tracks' names and their `hidden`/`muted`/`locked`/`solo` flags —
//! because the timeline's tracks stay on the timeline, holding whatever was
//! not selected. They copy each source track's `volume` and `pan` too, except
//! the copy of the nest clip's own destination track, which is unity and
//! centre: that track still applies over the nest clip, so a selection off one
//! track sounds the same nested, and the other source tracks' clips now also
//! get the destination's volume and pan on top of their own, as they get its
//! mute (spec §11 AU-102). `unnest` keeps neither, as it keeps no mute. A nested track keeps its source's `matte` only when the
//! nested track directly behind it is the copy of that matte's target (the
//! CARRY rule): a matte pair nested together goes on cutting inside the nest,
//! and a matte taken without its target becomes an ordinary track there.
//!
//! `unnest` lines the nest's front-most NON-matte track up with the nest
//! clip's track, so carried matte tracks land on the tracks in front of it
